  "tagline": "Colonial bungalows, villas and manors on a 30-acre Sentosa estate.",
  "description": "Capella Singapore is an urban resort on Sentosa Island with 113 rooms, suites, villas and manors in 30 acres of landscaped grounds, just over ten minutes from central Singapore. It opened on 30 March 2009.\n\nAt its heart are two Tanah Merah colonial bungalows from the 1880s, built for the Royal Artillery and since restored. The architects Foster + Partners designed the resort around them, taking Tanah Merah, Malay for red earth, as the starting point, and the interiors are by Jaya Ibrahim, combining contemporary comfort with natural materials and touches of Asian tradition.\n\nThe Premier Garden, Seaview and Constellation Rooms have large balconies, some with private Jacuzzis, and look over the grounds or the South China Sea. The 11 Sentosa and Capella Suites have separate living and sleeping areas, large balconies over the sea, Bamford toiletries and Bose sound systems. Each of the 38 villas has an outdoor terrace with a private plunge pool, and the One-Bedroom Villas sleep up to three adults or two adults and two children. The manors are the most private option: the two Colonial Manors sit in the restored 1880s buildings, and the Capella and Contemporary Manors stand on the edge of the estate, each with a terrace and a private pool and its own Manor Culturist on call day and night.\n\nFiamma serves Italian family cooking, with wood-fired pizzas, hand-made pastas and a Sunday Champagne Brunch. Cassia is a modern Cantonese restaurant designed by Andre Fu. The Pineapple Room, inside the heritage building, is the cocktail bar, and Bob's Bar looks over the skyline from a terrace, serving a rum made for the resort and Singapore dishes such as laksa and Hainanese chicken rice.\n\nAuriga Spa has nine treatment suites, including four for couples, a vitality pool, a herbal steam room and an ice fountain, and is open from 8 am to 10 pm. There is a fitness centre with Technogym equipment. Capella Culturists arrange experiences such as wet market tours, mahjong lessons and Peranakan tile painting.\n\nThe resort suits travellers who want a private estate with the city close by; Changi International Airport is 26 kilometres away, and one pet of up to 15 kilograms is allowed per room.",
